/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  unifacDortmund02_butanol_water_gamma_infinity -- A TABLE THE PAPER PRINTS,
  REPRODUCED TO ITS DIGITS.

  Gmehling, Li & Schiller, Ind. Eng. Chem. Res. 32 (1993) 178, Table XI (p. 192)
  prints the activity coefficient at infinite dilution of 1-butanol, isobutanol,
  2-butanol and tert-butanol IN WATER at 25, 50, 75 and 100 C, as predicted by
  the original UNIFAC (Hansen 1991) and by the modified UNIFAC (Dortmund) the
  paper presents.  This case asks the engine for the same 32 numbers with
  `model UNIFACDortmund;` and `model UNIFAC;`.

  THESE ARE PREDICTIONS, NOT MEASUREMENTS.  Table XI has no experimental
  column; what is anchored here is that THIS ENGINE REPRODUCES WHAT THE PAPER
  PRINTS -- the equations (r^(3/4) combinatorial, T-dependent Psi), the
  parameters it read from Tables III-IV, the OH_p/OH_s/OH_t split (1- and
  isobutanol are the same numbers by construction: equal r and q), and the
  untouched original UNIFAC.  The `anchor` rows of `expected` carry the printed
  values at their printed precision (3 significant figures: reltol 3e-3).  The
  measured comparison is unifacDortmund01_ethanol_water_reduced_pressure.

  gamma-infinity is evaluated as gamma at x_alcohol = 2.5e-9 each in water (the
  four alcohols are trace at once; the limit differs from the exact one by
  about one part in 1e8).  The paper's own remark applies: for all butanols
  the model still predicts a miscibility gap with these parameters.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

operations
(
    {
        name        dortmund_25C;
        type        propertyPoint;
        state       { T 298.15 K;  P 1 atm;  composition { nButanol 2.5e-9;  2Methyl1Propanol 2.5e-9;  2Butanol 2.5e-9;  2Methyl2Propanol 2.5e-9;  water 0.99999999; } }
        properties  ( gamma_nButanol  gamma_2Methyl1Propanol  gamma_2Butanol  gamma_2Methyl2Propanol );
    }
    {
        name        dortmund_50C;
        type        propertyPoint;
        state       { T 323.15 K;  P 1 atm;  composition { nButanol 2.5e-9;  2Methyl1Propanol 2.5e-9;  2Butanol 2.5e-9;  2Methyl2Propanol 2.5e-9;  water 0.99999999; } }
